   130	Sample preparation
   131	Before starting the test procedure all materials involved in the
   132	stages of analysis of this study were subjected to demineralization
   133	protocol (Nitric acid 20% for 24h) aiming at exclusion of possible
   134	contaminated materials.
   135	Samples were first wet acid digested in count tubes containing
   136	nitric acid (65%) and hydrogen peroxide on a digester block
   137	(Tecnal TE-040/25) at 150 °C for 5 days. After complete digestion
   138	of organic matter, the mineral content was transferred into a
   139	10 mL flask and the volume was made up with deionized water.
   140	A certified reference standard was also wet acid digested to ensure
   141	the sample preparation procedure was consistently applied.
   142	Analysis of minerals
   143	Analysis of the contents of calcium, iron, copper, manganese,
   144	zinc, barium, cadmium, chromium, lithium, molybdenum,
   145	phosphorus, lead and vanadium in the digested samples was
   146	performed using inductively Coupled Plasma Optical Emission
   147	
   148	Contents were expressed as means ± standard deviation
   149	(n=3). Inferential tests for normality (Shapiro-Wilks test) and
   150	for homogeneity of variances (Brown-Forsythe test) were run
   151	on Action v.2.5 software (Statcamp, Brazil). One-way ANOVA
   152	(homoscedastic data) or Welch-ANOVA (heteroscedastic data)
   153	was used to test differences among minerals from the 5 samples
   154	from Rio Grande do Sul State, Brazil, while Duncan’s post-hoc
   155	multiple comparison test was used to compare means. P-values
   156	below 5% were statistically significant (Granato et al., 2014).
   157	
   158	3 Results and discussion
   159	3.1 Mineral composition
   160	The mineral composition results (calcium, iron, copper,
   161	chromium, manganese, molybdenum, phosphorus and zinc) for
   162	the 5 dehydrated bee-pollen samples are presented in Table 2.
   163	Statistical analysis showed significant difference in mineral
   164	content among samples, even among the 3 samples (B, C and D)
   165	collected at the same apiary. The levels of minerals may vary
   166	according to geographical and botanical origin of pollen grains
   167	(Szczêsna, 2007; Carpes et al., 2009; Morgano et al., 2012;
   168	Taha, 2015); therefore, pollen in samples collected at the same
   169	apiary may have been harvested from different flowers over the
   170	sampling period.
   171	Bee pollen samples were richest in phosphorus (P) (grand mean
   172	content - 480 mg/100 g). ANVISA Resolution RDC No. 54 states
   173	a food is classified as a “high content food” if it is able to provide
   174	over 30% RDI (recommended daily intake) of a certain mineral
   175	per serving (100 g), and is classified as a “source food” if it is
   176	able to provide at least 15% of RDI per serving (100 g) (Brasil,
   177	2012). In this study, all 5 bee pollen samples fell into the “high
   178	content food” class and can be used as dietary sources of P by
   179	both men and women between 19 and 30 years (Brasil, 2005,
   180	2012). Carpes et al. (2009) have reported P contents in samples
   181	collected in Rio Grande do Sul State (Brazil) ranging from
   182	565 and 846 mg/100 g. In a more comprehensive study using bee
   183	pollen samples from twelve Brazilian States, including Rio Grande
   184	do Sul State, Morgano et al. (2012) have observed P contents
   185	between 218 and 816 mg/100 g. All samples in this study met
   186	the standard for P content (between 80 and 600 mg/100 g) set by
   187	the International Honey Comission (IHC) (Campos et al., 2008).
