    95	During the study, 25 samples of unbranded whitening creams (N = 25) were collected from three
    96	different local markets of Karachi i.e. (i) Sunday Market near Farooq e Azam, (ii) Paposh Market, and (iii)
    97	Boltan Market respectively. At these markets, the locally manufactured cosmetic products are sold by
    98	unauthorized local companies where quality control is largely ignored. Therefore, the consumers are mostly
    99	unaware of how they are exposed to various harmful heavy metals through the application of such cosmetic
   100	products. In this regard, 25 skin whitening creams samples were collected labeled properly with sample
   101	code, and then stored at room temperature for laboratory analysis.
   102	
   103	SAMPLE DIGESTION
   104	The collected skin whitening cream samples were subjected to the wet digestion method (16). Each
   105	sample was put into a china dish and then oven-dried at the temperature of 103°C in a hot air oven. After
   106	this, 1gm of the oven-dried sample was taken out and transferred into a 100ml digestion flask in which 10ml
   107	of concentrated HNO3 (67%) was added. The mixture was then heated until it became pale yellow or
   108	colorless indicating the completion of the sample digestion and then the sample was cooled at room
   109	temperature. After this, the sample was filtered through Whattman filter paper no. 42 then 1ml of the filtrate
   110	was transferred into the measuring cylinder and diluted up to 50ml with deionized water. Lastly, the 50ml
   111	diluted sample was again filtered through Whattman filter paper no. 42 and then the filtrate was transferred
   112	into labeled disposable bottles for further heavy metals analysis.
   113	
   114	HEAVY METAL ANALYSIS
   115	The selected heavy metals (Pb, Ni, Cr, As, and Cu) were analyzed in all the 25 digested samples of
   116	skin whitening creams by using Merck Super Nova60 kits. The experiment for each sample was repeated 3
   117	times and then the results were obtained in the form of mean values and standard deviation.
   118	
   119	RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
   120	The present study was conducted to assess the concentrations of five heavy metals i.e. Lead (Pb),
   121	Nickel (Ni), Chromium (Cr), Arsenic (As), and Copper (Cu) in unbranded whitening creams sold in local
   122	markets of Karachi. The standards given by the Federal food drug and cosmetic act given by FDA Canada
   123	were followed in this study (17). The concentrations of heavy metals found in all the 25 whitening creams
   124	Copyright © 2022 Authors. This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits
   125	unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.
   126	
   127	68
   128	
   129	Pak Euro Journal of Medical and Life Sciences. Vol. 5 No. 1
   130	
   131	samples are presented in Table I. The mean concentrations of heavy metals were found in the order Pb > Cr
   132	> Ni > Cu > respectively.
   133	
   134	LEAD
   135	Overall, the mean concentration of lead was found highest among all the heavy metals. The minimum
   136	and maximum levels of lead were detected in the range of 2.11-9.12 ppm with the highest value observed in
   137	sample P-1 and the lowest was detected in sample P-23 as given in (table I). It was found that in all the
   138	samples, the concentration of lead was crossing the permissible limit defined by FDA Canada i.e. 1 ppm.
   139	There are some studies conducted in different regions of Pakistan to report lead concentration in various
   140	cosmetic products which can pose a serious health threat to humans. A similar study was conducted in
   141	Quetta to assess heavy metals in locally available cosmetic products showed that the concentration of lead
   142	was much lower as compared to the present study (15). Another study conducted in KPK revealed that the
   143	mean concentration of Pb was much higher in cosmetic products i.e. 141.6 ± 0.016 ppm (14). It was also
   144	observed that the use of eye cosmetics such as Surma and Kohl containing lead has been linked with the
   145	higher levels of lead in the blood of women and children (9). Children and pregnant women are more
   146	vulnerable to lead toxicity because it can enter the placenta and damage the brain of the fetus or it can also
   147	transfer through lactating mothers and stored in the bones (18, 19). Exposure to lead was also associated
   148	with hormonal imbalances; miscarriages, infertility issues. Additionally lead has been also classified as a
   149	suspected carcinogen to humans (20).
   150	
   151	NICKEL
   152	Humans are exposed to a small amount of nickel mainly through the air, water, food, dust, and also
   153	through dermal contact with various products used for skin especially cosmetics (21, 22). Exposure to nickel
   154	can also cause health issues depending upon the route and level of exposure (23). Nickel can also be
   155	responsible for allergic reactions especially associated with severe dermatitis (21). In the present study, the
   156	minimum concentration of Ni was observed in sample P-22 (0.12ppm) while the maximum was observed in
   157	sample P-9 (1.21ppm). Overall the mean concentration and standard deviation of Ni in all 25 samples of
   158	local whitening creams were found to be 0.546±0.274 ppm. Research conducted in the Malaysian market to
   159	assess selected heavy metals concentration in skin lightening products showed that the mean concentration
   160	of Ni was found slightly higher in non-local products (0.207±0.15 mg/kg) as compared to the local skin
   161	lightning products 0.180±0.17 mg/kg (24). Some studies are also conducted in Pakistan to assess heavy
   162	metals in cosmetic products and scientific literature revealed that in KPK, the mean level of nickel in
   163	different cosmetic products was 0.674±0.002 μg/g (14). Another study conducted in local markets of Lahore
   164	disclosed that Ni was detected in higher concentrations i.e. 55.6821 μg/g in only one locally manufactured
   165	lipstick sample which was crossing the permissible limits defined by US-FDA and poses threat to human
   166	health (25). According to scientific evidence, a concentration of nickel less than 5mg/kg is considered to be a
   167	safe manufacturing practice (26). Therefore, in the present study, nickel concentration remained within safe
   168	limits.
   169	
