    13	The escalating presence of heavy metals (HMs) in the Panjkora River water and their impact on fish
    14	pose a significant challenge to both the ecological community and human health. Consequently, a
    15	study was conducted with the primary aim of elucidating their influence on human health-related
    16	issues. To address this, the concentrations of heavy metals, including arsenic (As), cadmium (Cd),
    17	iron (Fe), manganese (Mn), lead (Pb), and zinc (Zn), in both water and the fish species Crossocheilus
    18	diplocheilus were investigated across various locations within the study area. The quantification of
    19	HMs concentration was carried out utilizing an atomic absorption spectrophotometer. The highest
    20	concentration in water was found as 0.060 mg/L for Pb and lowest for Fe, whereas the highest
    21	concentration in fish was 2.028 mg/kg for Pb and lowest for As. Human health risk associated with fish
    22	eating was evaluated by using health risk indices (HRI) for non-carcinogenic health risks and targeted
    23	cancer risk (TR) for carcinogenic health risks. The values of the health risk index (HRI) were found
    24	greater than 1 except Fe (0.0792), Zn (0.782), and Mn (0.541). The highest mean HRI > 1 was recorded
    25	for As (62.99), Cd (26.85), and Pb (10.56). This implies that fish consumption from river Panjkora is
    26	not safe up to some extent. Similarly, the TR value for As, Cd, and Pb was found 2.8 ×10−2, 1.6 ×10−2,
    27	2.8 ×10−3 which showed cancer risk. There is a detected risk to human health associated with the
    28	consumption of fish from the Panjkora River. The government must implement adaptive measures
    29	to address this significant issue of water pollution in the study area. Additionally, there is a need for
    30	further extensive and prolonged research studies in this context.
    31	Heavy metals (HMs) are defined by having an elemental density (ED) exceeding 5 g ­cm3 and an atomic number
    32	(Z) surpassing 20, as stipulated by Aliand and Khan in 2018. These metals, acknowledged for their nonbiodegradable, cytotoxic, mutagenic, or carcinogenic properties, pose significant environmental contamination
    33	­risks1–3. The growth of manufacturing and agricultural industries has notably contributed to the contamination of
    34	water with metals, impacting both the environment and living ­organisms4,5. Previous researchers demonstrated
