    59	that various sources emit heavy metals by contaminating both freshwater and saltwater, thereby affecting aquatic
    60	­life6–8.
    61	Different metals that infiltrate ground and surface water exhibit detrimental effects on aquatic life and human
    62	health. For instance, lead (Pb), widely utilized in paints, explosives, protective coatings, building materials, and
    63	wine due to its density, malleability, and erosion resistance, is particularly ­concerning9. The pollution of river
    64	networks with heavy metals stands out as a major contemporary environmental challenge globally, resulting from
    65	a combination of socioeconomic and industrial ­activities10,11. In the context of fluvial environments, this pollution
    66	can arise from geological weathering, atmospheric deposition, or the release of industrial, domestic, urban, or
    67	agricultural ­discharges12,13. In three-quarters of urban areas in Asia, Africa, and Latin America, industrial or
    68	urban wastewater irrigation is a common ­reality14. In today’s modern world, environmental contamination is the
    69	biggest problem. Among other environmental pollutants, HMs are well-known and cause more worry because
    70	of their toxicity to aquatic and terrestrial l­ife15. Human activities have released a significant number of HMs
    71	into the ­environment16–19. However, HMs, are persistent and harm the health of living ­things20,21. HMs from
    72	mining operations agriculture, and industries discharged into rivers are trapped by sediments that transit and
    73	bio-magnify HMs causing severe diseases in fish and human ­beings22–26. The heavy metal poisoning of many
    74	inland water environments has grown recently, according to ­Otchere25. Even though 70% of the Earth’s surface
    75	is made up of water, freshwater resources are quickly running ­out26. Further reducing the availability of clean,
    76	fresh water sources is the rapid industrialization and lifestyle changes that have led to the contamination of these
    77	watery resources with a wide range of ­pollutants27.
    78	Drinking water contaminated with metals can have long-lasting and short-term impacts, including lowered
    79	immunity, oxidative stress, gastrointestinal ulcers, and even cancer. Mercury (Hg), Copper (Cu), Zinc (Zn),
    80	Arsenic (As), Chromium (Cr), Nickel (Ni), Cobalt (Co), Cadmium (Cd), Lead (Pb), and others are toxic HMs that
    81	can be ­dangerous28. Natural and manmade activities, HMs accumulation in fish increase throughout the world,
    82	and its potential threat to ­health29,30. Infected drinking water causes over 0.84 million people to die from diarrhea
    83	each year, according to a WHO report from 2018. Pakistan, the world’s sixth most populous nation, is likewise
    84	grappling with severe ­water31. While some of the HMs, such as Pb, Hg, and Cd, are biologically nonessential
    85	and highly poisonous to living things, low quantities of others are necessary for the growth and development of
    86	living things. Even necessary metals can become harmful if their concentration exceeds the allowable ­limit32–34.
    87	Similar to this, metals in sediments can come from a variety of sources, including air deposition, mining deposits,
    88	bedrock weathering and erosion, and industrial and agricultural ­effluents35,36. Contaminants that contain HMs
    89	constantly enter rivers from rapid urbanization, agricultural activities, and ­industrialization37.
    90	Environmental pollution stands as the primary threat to public health in Pakistan, as highlighted by ­Shakir38.
    91	The water bodies within the country frequently exhibit the presence of heavy metals (HMs) such as mercury
    92	(Hg), iron (Fe), copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), nickel (Ni), and arsenic (As). These metals
    93	have detrimental effects on aquatic life and become incorporated into the food chain, as indicated by ­Hussain39.
    94	While certain heavy metals like lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), and mercury (Hg) lack known biological functions in
    95	fish, others such as iron, zinc, and copper play essential roles in fish ­metabolism40. The primary sources of metals
    96	and metalloids, whether natural or anthropogenic, consistently introduce these substances into aquatic bodies,
    97	posing significant threats to human and ecological health due to their toxic ­nature41. The release of heavy metals
    98	from sediments into water and their accumulation in fish depend on factors such as the solubility of the metals
    99	and processes like adsorption or ­precipitation42. The escalating population ratio contributes to the increasing
   100	levels of heavy metals, exacerbated by mismanagement of solid waste, agricultural practices, mining activities,
   101	and sanitation issues in the study area, negatively impacting the quality of the Panjkora River. The current study
   102	focuses on assessing the concentration of heavy metals in water and fish samples from the Panjkora River, aiming
   103	to determine the potential health risks associated with heavy metal ingestion through fish consumption in the
   104	study area.
   105	
   106	Materials and methods
   107	Description of study area
   108	
   109	The study was carried out in River Panjkora, located in District Dir lower, which lies in the Hindukush range at
   110	71°, 31°–72°, 14′ east and 34°, 37°–35°, 07′ ­north43. It is around 2700 feet above sea level. It is considered a major
   111	lifeline of the area, originates from Kohistan in the district of Upper Dir, and flows southward to Lower Dir.
   112	The total population of 767,409, and most of the population is located on the bank of the river. Sewage,
   113	industrial, and agricultural wastewater are entering into rivers without treatment, these are the main sources of
   114	water pollution in the study area. Dir is split into the Upper Dir and Lower Dir districts (Fig. 1). The software
   115	ArcGIS (version 10.5) was used to draw the country, province, and detail study area discussed here. Dir’s
   116	topography is dominated by mountains and hills that are a part of the Hindukush ranges. The mountain ranges
   117	run from north to south and from northeast to southwest along the northern borders with Chitral District. The
   118	Panjkora River enters the district from the northeast and flows southwest along the Bajour Agency’s border
   119	until it joins the Swat River. Several streams in the Lower Dir and Dir River, the major stream of the Upper Dir,
   120	combine to form Panjkora River.

