    11	This study aimed to assess the human health risk of some toxic metals/metalloids [lead (Pb), mercury
    12	(Hg), cadmium (Cd), nickel (Ni), chromium (Cr), and arsenic (As)] on infants via consumption of the
    13	breast milk of women living in urban areas of Kermanshah city, west of Iran. After collecting milk
    14	samples, the carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic risk assessment as well as uncertainty analysis of
    15	toxic metal levels were carried out. The order of concentration of heavy metals/metalloids in the
    16	breast milk samples was Cr (41.07 ± 23.19) > Ni (19.25 ± 11.81) > Pb (11.5 ± 4.48) > As (1.96 ± 2.04) > Cd
    17	(.72 ± 0.42) > Hg (0.31 ± 0.26). The results revealed that the levels of Cr and Pb in the breast milk
    18	samples were exceeded the World Health Organization (WHO) tolerable daily intake. In the breast
    19	milk samples a high levels of one of the trace elements As, Cd, Cr, Pb, and Ni were observed (over
    20	73%) and in 40% of them the levels of Cr, Pb, Cd, As, and Ni were all above WHO tolerable daily intake.
    21	Moreover, the As-related point assessment of target risk factor (THQ) was higher than the allowable
    22	limit only for 1-month-old male neonates and 2-month-old female neonates (THQ > 1). In addition,
    23	Cr-related THQ scores were higher at all age and gender groups (THQ > 1). In conclusion, our findings
    24	suggest a potential risk of some metals for infants via the consumption of mothers’ breast milk.
    25	Breastfeeding is essential to maintaine a good growth of the ­child1. The World Health Organization recommends
    26	that mother to breastfeed a child up to 6 months of age and then feeding with complementary foods for up to
    27	2 ­years2. However, milk can transfer toxic elements from the mother to the baby via breastfeeding. The presence
    28	of these toxins does not necessarily cause health hazards in the infant, but since these hazards are biologically
    29	probable, they should be ­monitored3. The first stage of transmission of contaminants to the baby begins from the
    30	time of placement in the mother’s womb and continues until ­breastfeeding4. Toxic heavy metals are one of the
    31	main chemical pollutants that enter the environment (water, soil, and air) naturally or through human activities
    32	and thay may enter the human body through inhalation, food, and skin ­transmission5–7 These heavy metals have
    33	toxic effects on human health and exposure to them can cause diffrent ­diseases7. Compared to adults, the infants
    34	are more sensitive to metal toxicity due to the rapid growth of body tissues, especially their nervous ­system4.
    35	Among the heavy metals/metalloids, lead (Pb), cadmium (Cd), arsenic (As), and mercury (Hg) are of greater
    36	health importance to humans. These metals are not metabolized in the body which may lead to toxicity due
    37	to their accumulation in the ­tissues8,9. As one of the leading carcinogens, cadmium, transmit to humans body
    38	through water, food, inhalation, and ­smoking10. The average weekly Cd intake in European Food Safety Authority (EFSA) is set at 2.5 μg/kg11. Digestive absorption of Cd during lactation (37%) is higher than in adulthood
    39	(5%). Therefore, maternal exposure to Cd, even at low environmental concentrations, can lead to health risks,
    40	especially neurological changes and deformities in the infant’s ­bones10,12.
    41	Another heavy metal, lead, can also enter the body through water, food, inhalation, and the skin ­pathway5,13. If
    42	Pb consumed orally, about 5–10% of Pb is absorbed. This increases to 45% in ­children14. The neurological effects
