    68	carcinogenic ­compounds16. In 2010, the Joint FAO/WHO Expert Committee on Food Additives (JECFA) set a
    69	weekly intake of 1.2 μg/kg for ­As11.
    70	Humans are exposed to organic, inorganic, and metallic Hg mainly through the consumption of fish, medicinal and cosmetic compounds, and dental ­amalgam19. Mineral and elemental Hg are excreted mainly in the urine.
    71	Significant amounts of Hg are also excreted via sweat, tears, and breast ­milk20. JECFAsuggests a permissible
    72	weekly intake of 1.6 μg/kg for ­Hg21. Fetuses and infants are exposed to methylmercury, which is the most toxic
    73	form of Hg, that determines 5 μg/kg for total ­Hg22. Some studies have reported the presence of toxic metals in
    74	human milk around the ­world11,23–27.
    75	On the contrary, the low concentrations of metals in human milk were determined in some ­reports28–30. There
    76	is little research available on the health risk of toxic metals/metalloids on infants transmitted via the breast milk
    77	using Iranian population. Therefore, this study aimed to measure the concentrations levels as well as the human
    78	health risk (non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic risk assessment) of six heavy metals/metalloids (Pb, Hg, Cd, Ni,
    79	Cr, and As) on infants via the breast milk of women in Kermanshah city, located in the west of Iran. We hypothesize that elevated heavy metal concentrations may have non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic risks for neonates.
