   380	Manganese (Mn) and copper (Cu) levels (2.8-6.2 mg
   381	and 0.7-1.3 mg/100 g) fell within the range of 1.9-4.3 mg and
   382	0.5-1.8 mg/100 g reported by Carpes et al. (2009) and Morgano et al.
   383	(2012), respectively, for Brazilian bee-pollen samples from Rio
   384	Grande do Sul. Szczêsna (2007), Yang et al. (2013) and Taha (2015)
   385	have reported similar results (0.8-43 and 0.3-2.5 mg/100 g) in
   386	pollen samples from other countries. Each sample in this study
   387	can be classified as a “source food” of Cu and a “high content
   388	food” of Mn. Brazilian women are estimated to consume Mn at
   389	levels below the RDI (Panziera et al., 2011); therefore, the daily
   390	intake of bee pollen may contribute to preventing Mn deficiency.
   391	Zinc (Zn) contents were slightly lower (2.9-4.3 mg/100 g)
   392	than those found in samples collected in the same Brazilian
   393	state (3.7-8.2 mg/100 g) (Carpes et al., 2009) but were similar
   394	to those reported by Fuenmayor et al. (2014) for samples from
   395	Colombia (2.6-5.3 mg/100 g). Samples 1 and 5 are “source
   396	foods” of Zn under the ANVISA classification system (Brasil,
   397	2005; Brasil, 2012).
   398	Food Sci. Technol, Campinas, 36(3): 505-509, July-Sept. 2016
   399	
   400	The levels of chromium (Cr) and molybdenum (Mo) in the
   401	5 simples ranged from 0.2 to 4.2 mg and 0.02 to 0.46 mg/100 g,
   402	respectively. To our knowledge, only one study about these
   403	minerals in bee pollen samples has been published; Yang et al.
   404	(2013) observed Mo levels between 0.01 and 0.04 mg/100 g in
   405	Chinese bee pollen but did not detect any Cr in the samples.
   406	A 25g serving of bee pollen is enough to provide 100% of the
   407	RDI of Cr. Samples 2, 3 and 4 can be good sources of Mo and
   408	sample 1 can provide Mo levels twice as high as that of the RDI
   409	for men or women (19-30 years) (Brasil, 2005, 2012).
   410	3.2 Mineral contaminants
   411	The contents of inorganic contaminants (barium, cadmium,
   412	lithium, lead and vanadium) present in dehydrated bee pollen
   413	samples collected are shown in Table 3.
   414	Inorganic contaminants were found in all 5 bee pollen
   415	samples analyzed. Barium (Ba) was present at the highest
   416	levels (0.393-1.593 mg/100 g), comprising about 52% of total
   417	mineral contaminants. Morgano et al. (2010) found an average
   418	Ba content of 0.541 mg/100 g in 43 samples of bee-pollen from
   419	three Brazilian States (São Paulo, Minas Gerais and Espírito
   420	Santo). Ba compounds have many industrial applications and
   421	their toxicity depends on their solubility in water or in the gastric
   422	juice (Berman, 1980).
   423	To our knowledge, the presence of vanadium (V) and
   424	lithium (Li) in bee pollen samples has not been studied before.
   425	The essentiality of Vanadium for humans is still questioned, and
   426	there are not enough literature data to determine its nutritional
   427	index status. Some studies have reported that V can have a slowing
   428	effect on the metabolism of triacylglycerols and cholesterol.
   429	Although Vanadium is nutritionally important, it can be toxic
   430	if ingested at levels higher than 1.8 mg per day. A 25g-serving of
   431	any bee pollen sample in this study, thus, can be a suitable source
   432	of Vanadium. The improper disposal of products containing Li
   433	can contaminate soil and water and eating contaminated food
   434	can cause serious damage to health (Floridia & D’Agati, 2013).
   435	Cadmium (Cd) levels, 0.0026-0.0244 mg/100 g, were lower
   436	than those found by Yang et al. (2013) for bee-pollen samples
   437	from China (0.015-0.049 mg/100 g). Lead (Pb) contents
   438	(0.012-0.018 mg/100 g) fell within the range reported by Yang et al.
