   709	
   710	Health risk assessment
   711	
   712	Risks to human health via consumption of HMs contaminated fish were determined by using health risk indices
   713	EDI and HRI by using Eqs. 1, 2, and 3, respectively and their results are given in Tables 1, 2, and 3.
   714	The results of the estimated daily intake of HMs via contaminated fish are given in Table 1. The maximum daily
   715	intake of Fe, Pb, Mn, Zn, Cd, As were occurring at 0.08, 0.451, 0.105, 0.32, 0.04, 0.03 mg/kg/day in points E, E, D,
   716	and G, D, G, D while the minimum daily intake occurs of 0.03, 0.20, 0.048, 0.15, 0.02 and 0.008 in point C, G, B,
   717	G, C, C. So, it was clear from the result that the maximum daily intake of HMs occurred for Pb and Zn. Similarly,
   718	the daily intake of HMs in all sample points of the river Panjkora follows the order of Pb > Zn > Mn > Fe > Cd > As.
   719	The EDI values for Pb, Cd, and As were found higher than their corresponding reference doses. While the rest
   720	HMs have lower EDI than the reference dose, so they do not pose non-carcinogenic health risks in the short term.
   721	The health risk index (HRI) for individual HMs calculated from Eq. 2 and presented in the Table 2. The
   722	results showed that the mean HRI for Fe, Pb, Mn, Zn, Cd, and As were found 0.079 mg/kg/day, 10.56 mg/kg/
   723	day, 0.541 mg/kg/day, 0.782 mg/kg/day, 26.85 mg/kg/day and 62.91 mg/kg/day. HRI values for Fe ranged from
   724	(0.121–0.042) Pb (15–8.33) Mn (0.75–0.342) Zn (1.06–0.5) Cd (31–40) As (100.0–27.66). The highest mean HRI
   725	in all sample locations was recorded for As, Cd, and Pb. Overall, the HRI value of HMs decreased in the order
   726	of As > Cd > Pb > Zn > Mn > Fe. While the rest of the heavy metals Fe, Zn, and Mn have HRI < 1 while in point D,
