   574	
   575	During the manufacturing process of cosmetic products, hexavalent chromium which is a known
   576	human carcinogen can be added as an impurity and may cause allergic dermatitis due to exposure through
   577	skin contact from such products (29). Moreover, it is also reported that the elevated levels of chromium in
   578	urinary samples of pregnant women may be associated with chromium exposure through the use of
   579	cosmetics, passive smoking, and other sources (30). Exposure to hexavalent chromium may also lead to lung
   580	cancer through inhalation and can cause an increased risk of stomach tumors due to exposure through
   581	consuming chromium contaminated drinking water (31).
   582	
   583	ARSENIC
   584	In the present study, the concentration of arsenic in locally manufactured skin whitening creams was
   585	found in the range of 0.005-0.091 ppm with the lowest concentration observed in sample P-5 and the highest
   586	recorded in sample P-24 as shown in Table 1. The mean concentration of arsenic in all 25 samples of
   587	Copyright © 2022 Authors. This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits
   588	unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.
   589	
   590	70
   591	
   592	Pak Euro Journal of Medical and Life Sciences. Vol. 5 No. 1
   593	
   594	whitening creams was found at 0.022±0.021 ppm which showed that the levels of As in the samples were
   595	found within the safe limits i.e. <3ppm according to FDA Canada guidelines. Comparison of the results
   596	obtained with the previous studies showed that the levels of arsenic in cosmetic products sold in local
   597	markets of Iraq were found much higher i.e. in the range of 4.83-5.73 mg/kg (or ppm) compared to the level
   598	found in the present study (32). A study conducted to assess the concentration of heavy metals in various
   599	cosmetic products sold in Iran showed that arsenic levels were found highest (0.359–3.853 μg/g) compared
   600	to other heavy metals found in the samples (4). Moreover, a recent study conducted in Pakistan to assess
   601	heavy metals in colored cosmetic products also revealed that higher levels of arsenic were detected in eye
   602	shadow and lipsticks samples (33).
   603	Human exposure to arsenic may result in adverse health issues such as high blood pressure, skin
   604	lesions, gangrene, cancer, peripheral vascular disease, and melanosis (34). Long-term exposure to arsenic
   605	can also cause chronic diseases such as macrophage dysfunction due to deterioration of immune systems
   606	and also affecting lymphocytes (35). It was also reported that exposure to arsenic and UV light are the
   607	important risk factors that are associated with squamous and basal cell carcinoma (36).
   608	
   609	COPPER
   610	Exposure to copper through dermal contact may result in skin discoloration, dermatitis and can also
   611	cause throat or nose irritation (37). Furthermore, long term human exposure to copper can also cause
   612	behavioral and physiological disturbances (such as progressive demyelination and brain damage),
   613	psychiatric issues (including aggressive behavior, depression, and suicidal tendencies), and other health
   614	issues including motor dysfunction, liver cirrhosis and hemolytic anemia (37–39). In the present study, the
   615	results revealed that the minimum and maximum concentrations of copper in 25 local skin whitening
   616	creams sample were found to be 0.22 ppm in sample P-22 and 0.85 ppm in sample P-1. Only scantly
   617	literature is available related to copper analysis in cosmetic products and their related health effects due to
   618	exposure. A study conducted in Nigeria to assess heavy metals in personal care products reported that the
   619	highest concentration of copper was observed in hair cream i.e. 0.783 ppm (37). Similarly, copper
   620	concentrations in traditional cosmetics (henna and kohl) sold in local markets of Tunisia were found in the
   621	range of 3.7-90.0 μg/g in henna products and 2.5-162.5 μg/g in kohl products (40). However, in the present
   622	study, the mean concentration of Cu was found to be much lower compared to previous studies i.e.
   623	0.489±0.145 ppm which indicates that the samples are not associated with copper toxicity to the consumers.
   624	
   625	CONCLUSION
   626	The present study investigated for the first time to assess the concentration of selected heavy metals
   627	(Pb, Ni, Cr, As, and Cu) in unbranded skin whitening creams sold in local markets of Karachi, Pakistan. The
   628	results revealed that lead was found to be in the highest concentration in all the samples as compared to
   629	other heavy metals which are found in order of Pb>Cr>Ni>Cu>As respectively. It shows that the high level
   630	of heavy metals contamination in the samples is mainly due to the mismanaged manufacturing of such
   631	products without any proper monitoring of toxic metals during the production process. Quality control
   632	methods are not applied. Therefore, it is necessary to monitor and control the fate of heavy metals in
   633	cosmetic products, especially the unbranded ones to avoid human exposure to such toxic heavy metals.
   634	There are no safety regulation guidelines defined in the country to assess the heavy metals in cosmetic
   635	products which is another major drawback that needs attention. Moreover, further studies are need to be
   636	performed related to the daily use of cosmetic products contaminated with heavy metals to assess the
   637	exposure risk of humans to different toxic heavy metals. There is also an urgent need to define acceptable
   638	limits of potential pollutants in cosmetic products that must be enforced at local levels. Keeping in view that
   639	it is not possible to remove heavy metals from such demanding products after their manufacturing;
   640	therefore, safe manufacturing practices must be implemented during the production of cosmetic products so
   641	that the quality of products can be improved without compromising human health.
