   150	water. A total of fifteen samples were collected along the tributaries of river Panjkora. The samples were collected
   151	between March 2021 and April 2022. All samples were gathered in a 1-L bottle and preserved with 5 ml ­NHO3.
   152	Samples were sent to the "Advance Hydro-Geochemistry Laboratory, Department of Environmental Sciences,
   153	Quaid-I-Azam University Islamabad" for atomic absorption spectrophotometry. The samples were kept at 4 °C
   154	in the aforementioned laboratory and were examined within three ­months44.
   155	A 250 volumetric flask was filled with 100 ml of water to remove the turbidity, and 5 ml of ­NHO3 (55%)
   156	was added to increase the sample’s acidity. The acidified samples in the fume hood evaporated to a volume
   157	of 20 ml on a heated plate. After that, the samples were removed from the hot plate and left to cool at room
   158	temperature. The cooled samples received an additional 5 ml of ­NHO3, which was then again evaporated to
   159	produce 20 ml. After chilling at room temperature, the samples are diluted to 100 ml with tape water. Atomic
   160	absorption spectrophotometry (Spectra AA 2000) was employed to detect heavy elements like Zn, Pb, As, Cd,
   161	Fe, and Mg in the samples. To create the standard curves, characteristic standard solutions for each HMs were
   162	made and aspirated into a flame atomic absorption spectrophotometer (Spectra AA 2000). The curve is used
   163	to read the concentration of a particular HMs. To measure the concentration of HMs, ppm units were used.
   164	
   165	Fish sampling and analysis
   166	
   167	The sample of fish was collected from the seven locations of the river Panjkora by a skilled fisherman and
   168	washed several times with distilled water. The sample was sealed in plastic bags with an ice box and transported
   169	to the laboratory for further analysis. In the laboratory, the fish sample was dissected with stainless scissors and
   170	forceps. The muscle of the fish was cut into small pieces and then dehydrated in the oven at 80 °C to obtain a
   171	constant weight. The dry sample converts into a fine powder with the help of mortar and pestle and is stored in
   172	desiccators until digestion. After this, 1 g of the sample was digested with 6 ml of 60% ­HNO3 and 3 ml of 35%
   173	­H2O2 in a digestion flask for one hour. After that, the sample was cooled and filtered with Whatman filter paper
